# Xiaomi alioth (Redmi K40 / POCO F3).
#
# The touchscreen is the primary pointer on this device -- there is no stick
# to fall back on -- so tag it for libinput and seat0 explicitly rather than
# relying on the generic rules matching an SPI-attached panel.
#
# The calibration matrix is what makes touch land where the picture is.
# gamescope composites 2400x1080 onto a panel whose native mode is
# 1080x2400, and --force-composition-rotation only says *where* the rotation
# happens (compositor instead of scanout) -- it does not set an angle, so
# gamescope never learns the panel's orientation and never transforms touch.
# Measured on the device 2026-10-23: the picture was upright but touch was
# 90 degrees counter-clockwise from it, so no button could be hit and every
# tap or swipe read as a gesture ("it jumps back to the home page").
# Normalised touch (nx, ny) has to become (ny, 1 - nx), which is the libinput
# matrix below. Doing it here rather than with --force-orientation leaves the
# picture alone; --force-orientation would rotate an output that is already
# correct.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ATTRS{name}=="Focaltech FT8756 Touchscreen", \
  ENV{ID_INPUT}="1", ENV{ID_INPUT_TOUCHSCREEN}="1", TAG+="seat", TAG+="uaccess", \
  ENV{LIBINPUT_CALIBRATION_MATRIX}="0 1 0 -1 0 1"

# Power key. steamos-powerbuttond enumerates udev for input/event* and then
# takes only the device carrying STEAMOS_POWER_BUTTON -- those three strings
# ("event*", "input", "STEAMOS_POWER_BUTTON") are the whole of its matching
# logic. Nothing in the generic or sm8250 rules sets it on a PMIC pwrkey, so
# on alioth the daemon walked every event device and then **exited 0**: no
# error, not one line in the journal, and a power button that neither blanked
# the screen nor opened the power menu. Measured on the device 2026-10-23;
# with the property set it logs
#   Found power button device at /dev/input/event0
# pm8941_pwrkey is the PMIC power key (pm8150 pon@800); pm8941_resin next to
# it is volume-down, which InputPlumber claims instead -- matching on the
# name keeps the two apart.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ATTRS{name}=="pm8941_pwrkey", \
  ENV{STEAMOS_POWER_BUTTON}="1"
